{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.72,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_tolerance",
      "components": ["R35"],
      "pins": ["R35.1", "R35.2"],
      "nets": ["/BRB_{pH}", "GND"],
      "observed": "R35 is the BRB current-sense resistor for U5, but its component metadata states a 20% tolerance, while the other five current-sense resistors (R30-R34) are described as 1% and R35's own library part is also a 1% part.",
      "expected": "The spec describes R30-R35 as the TMC2209 current-sense resistor set, and sound practice for matched motor-driver current sensing is to use the same low-tolerance sense resistors (here 1%) across all channels.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"R35\") ... (description \"110 mOhms ±20% 0.333W, 1/3W Chip Resistor 0805 (2012 Metric) Current Sense Thin Film\") ... (field (name \"Tolerance\") \"20%\") ... (libsource ... (part \"RES 110mΩ ±1% 1/4W 0805 Susumu\") ...)"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"R34\") ... (description \"110 mOhms ±1% 0.333W, 1/3W Chip Resistor 0805 (2012 Metric) Current Sense Thin Film\") ...)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "each with its own current-sense resistors and a shared single-wire UART bus (`STEP_BUS`) ... current-sense resistor pair (R30–R35, 110 mΩ)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The pH pump BRB current-sense channel can be off by up to 20% relative to the other channels, causing an incorrect current limit/torque calibration for U5 and asymmetric dosing-pump behavior.",
      "confidence": 0.82
    },
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["U1"],
      "pins": ["U1.2"],
      "nets": ["unconnected-(U1-EN-Pad2)"],
      "observed": "The TPS62933 buck regulator enable pin is left unconnected/no_connect in the netlist.",
      "expected": "For the intended +24V-to-+5V rail to reliably power up, the EN pin should be driven high or connected to a defined pull-up/enable path unless the datasheet explicitly permits leaving it floating; the spec's power architecture assumes U1 produces +5V.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"136\") (name \"unconnected-(U1-EN-Pad2)\") (node (ref \"U1\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"EN\") (pintype \"input+no_connect\")))"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "U1 — TPS62933 (TI) | 24 V → 5 V synchronous buck regulator, 3 A ... SW node feeds inductor L1 into the board's main `+5V` rail"}
      ],
      "impact": "The 5V rail may fail to start or be noise-sensitive, preventing the ESP32, 3.3V LDO, isolator primary sides, and TMC logic from powering correctly.",
      "confidence": 0.58
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist is broadly consistent with the r2 specification, but it contains at least one clear component-identity deviation: R35 is marked as a 20% current-sense resistor while the other sense resistors and the library part are 1%. The floating U1 EN pin is a secondary concern because the buck regulator is intended to power the board."
}

